---
/* ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   BrandWall — an angled field of the real logo, in outline, drifting.

   Replaces the single large BrandMark that used to close the footer. That mark
   arrived directly under CtaClose's ASCII rendering of the *same* logo, so the
   page ended by showing one piece of artwork twice; this says the name as a
   moving texture instead of as a second static logo.

   Three things make it read as a field rather than as a list of logos:

     1. It is the ACTUAL logo, not the word set in Fraunces. The paths are
        lifted from public/assets/fwd-logo.svg and painted as stroke-only, so
        the outline is the true letterform of the mark — including the dot,
        which the display face has no equivalent for.
     2. The whole slab is rotated, so rows run on a diagonal.
     3. Adjacent rows travel in opposite directions at unrelated speeds.

   COVERING A ROTATED RECTANGLE
   Rotating a box leaves triangular gaps at the corners, so the slab has to
   overhang its container on every side. For a WxH box at angle t the rotated
   slab must measure at least:

       width  >= W*cos(t) + H*sin(t)
       height >= W*sin(t) + H*cos(t)

   The height term is the one that bites: a wide, short footer needs a
   surprisingly tall slab, because the footer's own WIDTH contributes to it.
   Measured at 2560px the stack needed 1274px against 929px of rows — three
   rows short, which is exactly the bare corner this is drawn to avoid. Both
   axes are now derived rather than guessed, and the row count is generous
   enough to cover the worst case at every breakpoint.

   Decorative in full: aria-hidden, no links, no text a reader needs. The
   footer's real name and contact details sit above it in markup.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
import { LOGO_PATHS, LOGO_VIEWBOX } from "../data/logoPaths";

interface Props {
  /** Rows in the slab. Extra rows are clipped; too few leaves bare corners. */
  rows?: number;
  /** Degrees the whole slab is rotated by. Negative tilts up to the right. */
  angle?: number;
  /* ------------------------------------------------------------------
     The wall was written for exactly one home: the ink footer, masked to
     its right-hand track. It is now also the backing for the home page's
     work band (ink, same idiom) and its hero (paper). Those surfaces want
     the same drifting field but not the same ink-and-right-edge
     treatment, so the two things that were hard-coded became props.

     `tone` picks the stroke colour: white on ink, navy on paper. A paper
     surface needs a much lower alpha to sit at the same visual weight,
     since dark-on-light carries further than light-on-dark at equal
     opacity, so the tone also scales every row's `dim`.

     `mask` picks where the field paints, and every option exists to keep
     the field OFF the type — which is the rule the footer set: measured at
     1440px its wall starts painting at 893px while every word in it ends
     by 555px, so the mark and the text never share space.

     "right" is the footer's own ramp, clearing the text column. "gutters"
     is the hero's: the headline starts at --pad-x, so the field is held to
     the outer margins. "edges" clears a wider middle, and "below" clears a
     horizontal band off the TOP — for a section whose head spans the full
     width, where no vertical ramp can help. "full" is an even vignette for
     a surface that is texture throughout. */
  tone?: "ink" | "paper";
  mask?: "right" | "edges" | "gutters" | "below" | "full";
  /** With mask="below", the % of the height kept clear at the top. */
  clearTop?: number;
  /** Multiplies every row's stroke alpha. For dialling one instance back. */
  opacity?: number;
}

/* Rows are laid out at their NATURAL height (see `flex: none` below) and the
   stack simply overhangs the slab, so this number only has to be large enough
   to cover the rotated diagonal at every breakpoint — the tallest case needing
   ~15 rows at mobile mark size, and more once the rows are full height rather
   than squeezed. Overshooting is close to free: every mark is a <use> of one
   defined symbol, so an extra row costs a handful of elements rather than
   another copy of ~3000 characters of path data. */
const {
  rows = 24,
  angle = -14,
  tone = "ink",
  mask = "right",
  opacity = 1,
  clearTop = 42,
} = Astro.props;

/* A LIGHT blue rather than the dark brand blue: --color-blue (#144667) is deep
   enough that, once it is knocked back to a ~0.07 effective alpha over white,
   what survives is its value and not its hue — the hero field read as plain
   grey. A lighter, more saturated blue (#4E86AE) keeps its colour at the same
   faintness, because there is far less black in it to wash out.

   Being lighter, it also carries less weight per unit alpha, so the tone alpha
   goes up from 0.42 to 0.62 to land at the same visual presence as the white
   stroke on ink. Written as an rgb triple because the stroke composes it with a
   per-row alpha below. */
const STROKE = tone === "paper" ? "78 134 174" : "255 255 255";
const TONE_ALPHA = tone === "paper" ? 0.62 : 1;
const DIM_SCALE = TONE_ALPHA * opacity;

/* Per-row character. `dur` values are deliberately not multiples of one
   another, so rows drift out of phase instead of locking into a pattern that
   repeats every couple of cycles.

   Opacity, by contrast, is now CONSTANT across rows. The alpha used to vary
   per row (0.10 to 0.24, better than 2x between neighbours) on the theory that
   it would break up mechanical banding. It did the opposite: because the rows
   are stacked and parallel, an alpha that changes row by row reads as stripes
   of light and dark ruled across the field, which is exactly the banding it
   was meant to avoid. Varied SPEED and alternating DIRECTION already stop the
   field from looking gridded, and they do it without touching weight. One
   alpha for every row makes the wall read as a single texture.

   Slow on purpose: this is a texture behind a footer, not a ticker anyone is
   meant to read. At these durations a mark takes the better part of two
   minutes to cross, which registers as drift rather than as travel. */
const DIM = 0.17;                 // the midpoint of the old per-row range
const DURATIONS = [96, 127, 81, 143, 108, 89, 134];

/* Copies per row. A row scrolls by exactly ONE copy per cycle, so the loop is
   seamless only while the copies still to the right of the window cover it:

       (REPS - 1) x copyWidth >= slabWidth

   Get this wrong and the row simply runs out of marks before it resets, which
   shows up as blank space sweeping through the field — measured at 1920px with
   four copies: 3398px of cover against a 3648px slab, 250px short (and 1466px
   short at 2560px).

   So REPS is derived rather than guessed, against the widest slab worth
   covering. Over-covering costs nothing: extra copies are clipped, and the
   cycle still travels exactly one copy however many there are. */
const PER_COPY = 5;
const COVER_PX = 2560 * 2.1;      // widest slab worth covering
const COPY_PX = PER_COPY * 227;   // 13rem mark + 1.2rem gap, at 16px root
const REPS = Math.max(3, Math.ceil(COVER_PX / COPY_PX) + 1);

/* One id per instance, so two walls on a page cannot collide over it. */
const markId = `bw-mark-${Math.random().toString(36).slice(2, 8)}`;
---

<div
  class:list={["bw", `bw-mask-${mask}`]}
  style={`--bw-stroke:${STROKE}; --bw-clear-top:${clearTop}%`}
  aria-hidden="true"
>
  {/* The mark, defined ONCE. Every tile below is a <use> of this, so the path
      data appears a single time in the document instead of once per tile —
      the difference between a few hundred bytes and a megabyte of duplicated
      curves at this row count. */}
  <svg class="bw-defs" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
    <symbol id={markId} viewBox={LOGO_VIEWBOX}>
      {LOGO_PATHS.map((d) => <path d={d} />)}
    </symbol>
  </svg>

  <div class="bw-slab" style={`--angle:${angle}deg`}>
    {Array.from({ length: rows }, (_, i) => {
      const dur = DURATIONS[i % DURATIONS.length];
      /* Direction alternates by index rather than being carried in the data,
         so adding a row can never accidentally give two neighbours the same
         direction. */
      const reverse = i % 2 === 1;
      return (
        <div class="bw-row" style={`--dur:${dur}s; --dim:${(DIM * DIM_SCALE).toFixed(4)}; --reps:${REPS}`}>
          <div class:list={["bw-track", reverse && "is-reverse"]}>
            {Array.from({ length: REPS }, () => (
              <div class="bw-copy">
                {Array.from({ length: PER_COPY }, () => (
                  <svg class="bw-mark" viewBox={LOGO_VIEWBOX} fill="none" focusable="false">
                    <use href={`#${markId}`} />
                  </svg>
                ))}
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
        </div>
      );
    })}
  </div>
</div>

<style>
  .bw {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    user-select: none;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  /* Fades out towards the left, where the footer's real content sits, so the
     field never competes with the links for legibility. The layout reserves
     an empty right-hand track for this, and the ramp is aligned to it: gone
     by the time it reaches the text column, at full strength across the
     space kept clear for it. */
  .bw-mask-right {
    -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(90deg, transparent 62%, #000 86%, #000 100%);
    mask-image: linear-gradient(90deg, transparent 62%, #000 86%, #000 100%);
  }

  /* For a band whose content runs the full width: strongest at the two outer
     edges, cleared out of the middle where the type is. */
  .bw-mask-edges {
    -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(90deg, #000 0%, transparent 28%, transparent 72%, #000 100%);
    mask-image: linear-gradient(90deg, #000 0%, transparent 28%, transparent 72%, #000 100%);
  }

  /* Only the outer gutters. The hero's headline starts at --pad-x and runs
     most of the column, so the "edges" ramp above (clear from 28%) still put
     marks under the first two or three characters of every line. This clears
     by 11%, which is inside the page gutter at every breakpoint, so the field
     frames the hero instead of sitting behind the type. */
  .bw-mask-gutters {
    -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(90deg, #000 0%, transparent 11%, transparent 89%, #000 100%);
    mask-image: linear-gradient(90deg, #000 0%, transparent 11%, transparent 89%, #000 100%);
  }

  /* Clears a band off the TOP instead of off the sides. The work band's
     section head is a two-column grid spanning the full width — title left,
     paragraph right — so there is no horizontal ramp that can miss it. A
     vertical one can: the field starts below the head and fills the space the
     work stage sits in, which is mostly empty ink. Combined with a gentle
     side fade so it still has no hard edge of its own. */
  .bw-mask-below {
    -webkit-mask-image:
      linear-gradient(180deg, transparent var(--bw-clear-top), #000 calc(var(--bw-clear-top) + 14%)),
      linear-gradient(90deg, #000 0%, rgb(0 0 0 / 0.35) 30%, rgb(0 0 0 / 0.35) 70%, #000 100%);
    mask-image:
      linear-gradient(180deg, transparent var(--bw-clear-top), #000 calc(var(--bw-clear-top) + 14%)),
      linear-gradient(90deg, #000 0%, rgb(0 0 0 / 0.35) 30%, rgb(0 0 0 / 0.35) 70%, #000 100%);
    -webkit-mask-composite: source-in;
    mask-composite: intersect;
  }

  /* An even vignette — texture everywhere, softened off the edges so the
     field has no hard boundary of its own. */
  .bw-mask-full {
    -webkit-mask-image: radial-gradient(ellipse 85% 85% at 50% 50%, #000 20%, transparent 88%);
    mask-image: radial-gradient(ellipse 85% 85% at 50% 50%, #000 20%, transparent 88%);
  }

  /* Carries the <symbol> only; nothing in it paints directly. */
  .bw-defs { position: absolute; width: 0; height: 0; }

  /* Oversized on both axes — see the covering note at the top of this file.
     210% carries the height term (W*sin + H*cos) for a wide, short footer,
     which is the case that was leaving a bare corner at 210%'s predecessor. */
  .bw-slab {
    position: absolute;
    top: 50%;
    left: 50%;
    width: 210%;
    height: 210%;
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%) rotate(var(--angle));
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: clamp(0.15rem, 0.5vw, 0.5rem);
  }

  /* flex: none is load-bearing. The slab is a column flex container and the
     rows deliberately overflow it, so with the default `flex: 0 1 auto` every
     row SHRANK to fit — 113px of mark squeezed into a 58px row.

     overflow is visible, NOT hidden, and that matters just as much. The mark
     is an italic script: its ascenders and the tall `d` paint above the box
     the <svg> reserves, so a row that clips to its own height beheads every
     letterform. Nothing needs clipping at this level anyway — the row is as
     wide as its content and .bw already clips the whole field to the footer,
     which is the only edge that should ever cut a mark. */
  .bw-row {
    flex: none;
    overflow: visible;
  }

  /* max-content, not the flex default: the keyframe moves by a percentage of
     this box, so the box has to measure its content rather than the row it
     overflows. */
  .bw-track {
    display: flex;
    width: max-content;
    animation: bwDrift var(--dur) linear infinite;
  }
  /* Reversing the direction rather than writing a second keyframe keeps both
     directions travelling exactly one copy per cycle, so a left row and a
     right row stay in step at the same duration. */
  .bw-track.is-reverse { animation-direction: reverse; }

  .bw-copy {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    flex: none;
    gap: var(--bw-gap);
    padding-right: var(--bw-gap);
  }

  /* Outline only. The logo's own paths, stroked rather than filled — so this
     is the true contour of the mark, not an approximation in a text face.
     vector-effect keeps the hairline at 1px however the mark is scaled. */
  .bw-mark {
    flex: none;
    width: clamp(7rem, 13vw, 13rem);
    height: auto;
    stroke: rgb(var(--bw-stroke) / var(--dim));
    stroke-width: 1;
    fill: none;
    vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke;
    overflow: visible;
  }
  .bw-row { --bw-gap: clamp(0.6rem, 1.2vw, 1.2rem); }

  @keyframes bwDrift {
    to { transform: translateX(calc(-100% / var(--reps))); }
  }

  /* Paused by the footer's own control, so one button stops the whole field.
     The home page's hero and work band reuse the same hook on their own
     wrappers, so any surface can stop its wall by toggling one class. */
  :global(.ft.is-paused) .bw-track,
  :global(.is-paused) .bw-track { animation-play-state: paused; }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .bw-track { animation: none; }
  }
</style>
